Sending keys: ["cat > /app/output/answer.txt <<'EOF'\n## Statute\n§1(b) - Tax imposed on heads of households\n§2(b) - Definition of head of household\n§151(a), (c), (d) - Personal exemptions\n§152(d)(1)(B) - Qualifying relative income test\n§63 - Taxable income defined\n\n## Reasoning\nAlice qualifies as head of household under §2(b) because she is unmarried, maintains a household for more than half the year where her father Bob lives, and pays over half the household costs (75%). Bob qualifies as her dependent under §152 because he is her father (§152(d)(2)(C)) and has no income (§152(d)(1)(B)).\n\nAlice is entitled to two personal exemptions of $2,000 each under §151: one for herself and one for Bob as her dependent.\n\nTaxable income calculation:\n- Gross income: $67,285\n- Less itemized deductions: -$17,817\n- Less personal exemptions (2 × $2,000): -$4,000\n- Taxable income: $45,468\n\nUnder §1(b)(ii), head of household tax is $4,440 plus 28% of excess over $29,600:\n- Excess: $45,468 - $29,600 = $15,868\n- 28% of excess: $15,868 × 0.28 = $4,443.04\n- Total tax: $4,440 + $4,443.04 = $8,883.04\n\nRounded to whole number: $8,883\n\n## Answer\nAnswer: 8883\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
